# Exploring a cycle of absence tests
#
# Alice is a person. One rule puts a person :In when they are not known to
# be :Out; the other puts them :Out when they are not known to be :In. Each
# rule depends on the absence of what the other derives, so there is no
# order in which to settle them. Eyeron fires the first rule, concludes
# that Alice is :In, and the second rule is then blocked. It shows scoped
# negation (log:notIncludes) in a negative cycle, whose proof keeps the
# absence as an obligation.

@prefix log: <http://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/log#> .
@prefix : <https://eyereasoner.github.io/eyepl/examples/unstratified-negation#> .

:alice a :Person .

# A person not known to be out is in.
{
    ?x a :Person .
    ?SCOPE log:notIncludes {
        ?x a :Out
    } .
} => {
    ?x a :In
} .

# A person not known to be in is out.
{
    ?x a :Person .
    ?SCOPE log:notIncludes {
        ?x a :In
    } .
} => {
    ?x a :Out
} .
